# {{team}} — runtime entry (thin pointer)

Team handle: `{{team}}` · archetype `{{archetype}}`{{shape_note}} · repo `{{target_repo}}`

Load the **`team-forge:run`** skill and follow its `references/{{loop_ref}}` loop
for team `{{team}}`. Every fact is read LIVE at launch — never from this file.

**Resolve the hub from the main checkout, not your CWD** —
`dirname $(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)`. A linked worktree
receives no gitignored files, so `tracker/status.json` is *absent* there, not stale.
Paths below are relative to that root:

- `.claude/team-forge/{{team}}/tracker/status.json` — the single runtime surface:
  live state + the authoritative `goal_directive`, plus a design-derived `plan`
  block (gates, task/queue definitions, gate backing). Read `plan`, never edit
  it — `--resync` re-bakes it from design.yaml.
- `.claude/team-forge/{{team}}/design.yaml` — project, constraints, shape, profiles
- `docs/team-forge/{{team}}/contract.yaml` — the problem contract this runtime serves

> **Same runtime, other entry:** `claude --agent {{team}}-lead` runs this exact loop as an
> agent with persistent memory across runs — worth it when the team runs repeatedly. This
> slash command starts cold every time.

> **Staleness:** only this pointer + `gen_dashboard.py` are baked copies. On
> template drift (`tools/forge.py --check .claude/team-forge/{{team}}/design.yaml`),
> `--resync` regenerates them and preserves the ledger. The runtime policy itself
> lives in the plugin's `team-forge:run` and updates with the plugin.
